              %li
                %a{:href => "#References"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#general"} General
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#convolution_to_bimodules"}
                    Convolution to
                    = succeed "-bimodules" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi C
                              %mo *
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\ast
          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em bibundle
            is a (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid-principal+bundle"}> groupoid
            \-)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            which is equipped with a compatible second (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+action"}> groupoid
            \-)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            “from the other side”.
          %p
            In particular, Lie groupoid bibundles serve to exhibit “generalized morphisms”/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+morphisms"} Morita morphisms
            between
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoids"} Lie groupoids
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+stack"} differentiable stack
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+groupoid"} smooth groupoid
            represented by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} Lie groupoid
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid-principal+bundles"} groupoid-principal bundles
          %p
            Therefore groupoid bibundles play a role in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            analogous to the role played by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodules"} bimodules
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Haefliger"} Haefliger 84
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#HilsumSkandalis87"} Hilsum-Skandalis 87
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Pradines89"} Pradines 89
            = succeed "." do
              %em Hilsum-Skandalis maps
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#Bunge"}> Bunge 90
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Moerdijk91"} Moerdijk 91
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+convolution+algebra"} groupoid convolution algebra
            construction sends smooth bibundles between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoids"} Lie groupoids
            to (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+bimodule"}> Hilbert-
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodules"} bimodules
            of the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star-algebra"} C-star
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convolution+algebras"} convolution algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+equivalence"} Morita equivalence
            is respected (
            %a{:href => "#MuhlyRenaultWilliams"}> Muhly-Renault-Williams 87
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Landsman"} Landsman 00
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Mrcun"} Mrčun 05
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
            of Lie groupoids (
            %a{:href => "#HilsumSkandalis87"}> Hilsum-Skandalis 87
            ).
          %p
            Bibundles also appear as
            %em transition bundles
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+bundle+gerbes"} nonabelian bundle gerbes
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#LieGroupoidBibundlesAndMoritaMorphisms Lie groupoid bibundles and Morita/stack morphisms
          %p
            We discuss how Lie groupoid bibundles correspond to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+morphism"} Morita morphism
            (morphisms of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+stacks"} differentiable stacks
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+stacks"} smooth stacks
          %p First we set up the relevant definitions and establish our notation in
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#LieGroupoidsAndSmoothStacks"} Lie groupoids and smooth stacks
          %p
            Then we discuss smooth groupoid-principal bundles and how a Lie groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            for the bundles principal over it in
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#SmoothGrooupoidPrincipalBundles"}> Smooth groupoid-principal bundles
              \.
          %p Finally we consider the corresponding smooth bibundles and how they correspond to their modulating stack morphisms in
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#SmoothGroupoidPrincipalBibundles"} Smooth groupoid-principal bibundles
          %h4#LieGroupoidsAndSmoothStacks Lie groupoids and smooth stacks
          %p
            A
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+stack"} smooth stack
            or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+groupoid"} smooth groupoid
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            on the site
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SmoothMfd"} SmoothMfd
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            or equivalently (and often more conveniently) on its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+subsite"} dense subsite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
            of just
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+spaces"} Cartesian spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n, n \in \mathbb{N}
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            between them, equipped with the standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"} coverage
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/good+open+covers"} good open covers
          %p We write
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;\;\;
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SmoothGrpd"} SmoothGrpd
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi Sh
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi CartSp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi lhe
                  %mi Func
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi CartSp
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Grpd
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \coloneqq Sh_{(2,1)}(CartSp) \simeq L_{lhe} Func(CartSp^{op}, Grpd)
          %p
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C1%29-category"} (2,1)-category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stacks"} stacks
            on this site, equivalently the result of taking
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
            and then
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+localization"} universally
            turning local (as seen by the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"} coverage
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalences+of+groupoids"} equivalences of groupoids
            into global
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} equivalence in an (infinity,1)-category
          %p
            By generalizing here
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
            to general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complexes"} Kan complexes
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalences+of+groupoids"} equivalences of groupoids
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalences"} homotopy equivalences
            of Kan complexes, one obtains
            %em
              smooth
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stacks"} ∞-stacks
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} smooth ∞-groupoids
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;\;\;
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Smooth%E2%88%9EGrpd"} Smooth∞Grpd
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≔
                    %msub
                      %mi Sh
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi CartSp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mi lhe
                    %mi Func
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi CartSp
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi KanCplx
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \coloneqq Sh_{(\infty,1)}(CartSp) \simeq L_{lhe} Func(CartSp^{op}, KanCplx)
          %p
            We often write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo ≔
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H} \coloneqq
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Smooth%E2%88%9EGrpd"} Smooth∞Grpd
            for short.
          %p
            By the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-Yoneda+lemma"} (∞,1)-Yoneda lemma
            there is a sequence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} faithful inclusions
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;\;\;
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SmoothMfd"} SmoothMfd
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ↪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hookrightarrow
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SmoothGrpd"} SmoothGrpd
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ↪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hookrightarrow
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Smooth%E2%88%9EGrpd"} Smooth∞Grpd
          %p
            This induces a corresponding inclusion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+objects"} simplicial objects
            and hence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+objects+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} groupoid objects
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi LieGrpd
                  %mo ↪
                  %msub
                    %mi Grpd
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi SmoothMfd
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↪
                  %msub
                    %mi Grpd
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Smooth
                  %mn ∞
                  %mi Grpd
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  LieGrpd \hookrightarrow Grpd_\infty(SmoothMfd)  \hookrightarrow  Grpd_\infty(Smooth\infty Grpd)
                  \\,.
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒢
                    %mo •
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi Grpd
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_\bullet \in Grpd_\infty(\mathbf{H})
            a groupoid object we write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒢
                    %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒢
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mo ⟶
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒢
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{G}_0 \to \mathcal{G} \coloneqq \underset{\longrightarrow}{\lim}_{n} \mathcal{G}_n
          %p
            for its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-colimit"} (∞,1)-colimiting
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocone"} cocone
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒢
                  %mo ∈
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G} \in \mathbf{H}
            (without subscript decoration) denotes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/realization"} realization
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒢
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_\bullet
            as a single object in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mo •
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi Grpd
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_\bullet \in Grpd_\infty(\mathbf{H})
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+object+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} groupoid object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒢
                    %mo ≔
                    %msub
                      %mi lim
                      %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G} \coloneqq {\lim}_{n} \mathcal{G}_n \in \mathbf{H}
              for its
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/realization"} realization
              and call the canonical
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-epimorphism"} 1-epimorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒢
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{G}_0 \to \mathcal{G}
            %p
              the canonical
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atlas"} atlas
              of this realization.
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mo •
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Grpd
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi SmoothMfd
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %msub
                      %mi Grpd
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Smooth
                    %mn ∞
                    %mi Grpd
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_\bullet \in Grpd(SmoothMfd) \hookrightarrow Grpd_\infty(Smooth\infty Grpd)
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} Lie groupoid
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒢
                          %mn 0
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi SMoothMfd
                        %mo ↪
                        %mi Smooth
                        %mn ∞
                        %mi Gprd
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_0 \in SMoothMfd \hookrightarrow Smooth\infty Gprd
                  is its
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
                  of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒢
                        %mo ∈
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G} \in
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SmoothGrpd"} SmoothGrpd
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ↪
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hookrightarrow
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Smooth%E2%88%9EGrpd"} Smooth∞Grpd
                  is the realization of the Lie groupoid as a
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+stack"} differentiable stack
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+groupoid"} smooth groupoid
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒢
                          %mn 0
                        %mo →
                        %mi 𝒢
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_0 \to \mathcal{G}
                  is the canonically induced
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atlas"} atlas
                  in the traditional sense of
                  = succeed "-theory." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+stack"} geometric stack
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              By the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giraud-Rezk-Lurie+axioms"} Giraud-Rezk-Lurie axioms
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
              this morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒢
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_0 \to \mathcal{G}
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-epimorphism"} 1-epimorphism
              and its construction establishes is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} equivalence of (∞,1)-categories
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Grpd
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %msubsup
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi H
                        %mrow
                          %mn 1
                          %mi epi
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi Δ
                            %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Grpd_\infty(\mathbf{H}) \simeq \mathbf{H}^{\Delta^1}_{1epi}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mo •
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒦
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_\bullet \to \mathcal{K}_\bullet
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Grpd
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Grpd_\infty(\mathbf{H})
              are equivalently
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagrams"} diagrams
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
              of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi 𝒢
                              %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi 𝒦
                              %mn 0
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⇙
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi 𝒢
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi 𝒦
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\mathcal{G}_0 &amp;\to&amp; \mathcal{K}_0
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;\swArrow&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    \\mathcal{G} &amp;\to&amp; \mathcal{K}
                    }
                    \\,.
          %p This is evidently more constrained than just morphisms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒢
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒦
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{G} \to \mathcal{K}
          %p
            by themselves. The latter are the
            %em generalized morphisms
            or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+morphisms"} Morita morphisms
            between the groupoid objects
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_\bullet
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒦
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}_\bullet
          #MoritaMorphism.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Given
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+objects+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} groupoid objects
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒢
                        %mo •
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒦
                        %mo •
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi Grpd
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_\bullet, \mathcal{K}_\bullet \in Grpd_\infty(\mathbf{H})
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+morphism"} Morita morphism
              between them is a morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒢
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G} \to \mathcal{K}
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
              between their
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/realizations"} realizations
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} equivalence
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
              is called a
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+equivalence"} Morita equivalence
              of groupoid objects in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
          %p
            Here we want to express these Morita morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒢
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒦
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G} \to \mathcal{K}
            in terms of bibundle objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mo ∈
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P} \in \mathbf{H}
            on which both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒢
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_\bullet
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒦
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}_\bullet
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} act
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in \mathbf{H}
              any
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair+groupoid"} pair groupoid
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Pair
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi Grpd
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pair(X)_\bullet \in Grpd_\infty(\mathbf{H})
              is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Pair
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi n
                    %mo ≔
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mo ×
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Pair(X)_n \coloneqq X^{\times^{n+1}}
                    \\,.
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/realization"} realization
              of this is equivalent to the point
            .maruku-equation
